{
  "id": "c10",
  "title": "Arrays",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c10-l1",
      "title": "Many Values, One Name",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "An array holds many values of the same type under one name. int temp[7]; makes room for seven ints: temp[0] to temp[6].",
        "C counts from 0. The first element is temp[0], the last one temp[6]. Using temp[7] is outside the array, and C doesn't stop you: it just reads or writes some other memory. Bugs like that crashed many Unix programs.",
        "You can fill an array right away: int days[4] = {31, 28, 31, 30}; With empty brackets, C counts the values for you: int days[] = {31, 28, 31, 30};"
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int days[4] = {31, 28, 31, 30};",
          "    printf(\"%d\\n\", days[0]);",
          "    printf(\"%d\\n\", days[3]);",
          "    days[1] = 29;",
          "    printf(\"leap: %d\\n\", days[1]);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "31",
          "30",
          "leap: 29"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c10-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is the first element of int a[5]?",
          "options": [
            "a[0]",
            "a[1]",
            "a[5]",
            "a"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "C counts from 0."
        },
        {
          "id": "c10-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is the last element of int a[5]?",
          "options": [
