    {
      "id": "c10-l2",
      "title": "Loops over Arrays",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Arrays and for loops belong together: for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) visits every index of a 5-element array once. Note the < instead of <=.",
        "The same loop can fill the array, add up its values, or print them.",
        "#define N 5 at the top gives the size a name. Change it once, and every loop follows."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "#define N 5",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int a[N];",
          "    int i, sum = 0;",
          "    for (i = 0; i < N; i++)",
          "        a[i] = i * i;",
          "    for (i = 0; i < N; i++) {",
          "        printf(\"%d \", a[i]);",
          "        sum += a[i];",
          "    }",
          "    printf(\"\\nsum %d\\n\", sum);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "0 1 4 9 16 ",
          "sum 30"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c10-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why does the loop use i < N and not i <= N?",
          "options": [
            "The last index is N - 1",
            "It's faster",
            "<= isn't allowed",
            "To skip the first element"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "With <=, the loop would touch a[N], one past the end."
        },
        {
          "id": "c10-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    int a[3] = {2, 4, 6};",
            "    int i, s = 0;",
            "    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)",
            "        s += a[i];",
            "    printf(\"%d\\n\", s);",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "options": [
            "12",
            "6",
            "3",
            "9"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "2 + 4 + 6 = 12."
        },
        {
          "id": "c10-l2-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Print every element.",
          "code": [
            "    for (i = 0; i ___ 10; i++)",
            "        printf(\"%d\\n\", a[i]);"
          ],
          "options": [
            "<",
            "<=",
            ">",
            "=="
          ],
          "answer": "<",
          "explanation": "Indexes 0 to 9."
        },
        {
          "id": "c10-l2-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does #define N 5 do?",
          "options": [
            "Replaces every N with 5 before compiling",
            "Creates a variable N",
            "Makes an array",
            "Prints 5"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The preprocessor swaps the name for the value, like a search and replace."
        },
        {
          "id": "c10-l2-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program crashes. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    int a[4] = {1, 2, 3, 4};",
            "    int i;",
            "    for (i = 0; i <= 4; i++)",
            "        printf(\"%d\\n\", a[i]);",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "answer": 5,
          "explanation": "i <= 4 reaches a[4], which is outside the array. It must be i < 4."
        },
        {
          "id": "c10-l2-e6",
          "type": "order_lines",
          "prompt": "Put the lines in order: fill an array with 1, 2, 3 and print the last one.",
          "lines": [
            "int main() {",
            "    int a[3], i;",
            "    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)",
            "        a[i] = i + 1;",
            "    printf(\"%d\\n\", a[2]);",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "answer": [
            "int main() {",
            "    int a[3], i;",
            "    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)",
            "        a[i] = i + 1;",
            "    printf(\"%d\\n\", a[2]);",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "explanation": "Declare, fill in a loop, then print."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c10-l3",
      "title": "Biggest, Smallest, Average",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "To find the biggest value, start with the first element as the best so far, then compare it with every other element.",
        "Keep the index too, and you know where the biggest value was, like which day was the hottest.",
        "For the average, add everything up and divide by the count, as a double."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    double t[7] = {12.5, 15.0, 17.5, 21.0, 19.5, 16.0, 14.5};",
          "    int i, best = 0;",
          "    double sum = 0;",
          "    for (i = 0; i < 7; i++) {",
          "        sum += t[i];",
          "        if (t[i] > t[best])",
          "            best = i;",
          "    }",
          "    printf(\"hottest: day %d, %.1f\\n\", best + 1, t[best]);",
          "    printf(\"average: %.2f\\n\", sum / 7);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "hottest: day 4, 21.0",
          "average: 16.57"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c10-l3-e1",
